Unit 1: Introduction to Object Oriented Programming (3 Hrs.) 
============================================================
1.1 Overview of structured programming approach 
1.2 Object oriented programming approach 
1.3 Characteristics of object oriented languages 

1.1 Overview of structured programming approach
===============================================
Structured programming is a programming paradigm designed to improve clarity, reduce complexity, 
and make programs easier to debug and maintain. It focuses on writing code in a logical, 
top-down, and modular way using well-defined control structures.

Core Idea
=========
Break a program into small, manageable blocks and execute them in a clear sequence using 
structured control flow.

Main Principles
===============
1. Top-down design
==================
Problem is divided into smaller sub-problems
Each sub-problem is solved step by step
Example: “Build a bank system” → login → transactions → reports

2. Modular programming
======================
Program is divided into functions or modules
Each function performs a specific task
Improves reuse and readability

3. Structured control flow (3 basic constructs)
===============================================
a) Sequence
===========
Statements execute one after another

a = 5;
b = 10;
c = a + b;

b) Selection (Decision making)
==============================
Uses conditions:

if (a > b) {
    cout << "A is greater";
} else {
    cout << "B is greater";
}

c) Iteration (Loops)
====================
Repeats execution:

for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    cout << i << endl;
}

Features of Structured Programming
==================================
Easy to understand
Easier debugging and testing
Reduces complexity
Avoids chaotic “spaghetti code”
Encourages clean design

Advantages
==========
Better readability
Easier maintenance
Reusable code (functions)
Fewer logical errors

Limitations
===========
Not suitable for very large complex systems alone
No direct support for real-world modeling (objects)
Less flexible compared to OOP

Languages supporting structured programming
===========================================
C
Pascal
Ada
C++ (supports both structured + OOP)
Java (mostly OOP, but structured concepts exist)

Simple analogy
==============
Think of structured programming like:
Following a recipe step-by-step, instead of doing everything randomly.


1.2 Object-oriented programming approach
========================================
Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming approach where software is designed using 
objects and classes, just like real-world entities. It focuses on organizing code around data
(objects) rather than just functions.

Core Idea
=========
“Everything is an object that contains data and behavior together.”

Main concepts of OOP
====================
Real-world modeling
Code reusability
Security (data hiding)
Flexibility
Easy maintenance

Advantages
==========
Reduces complexity
Reusable code (inheritance)
Scalable systems
Better organization

Limitations
===========
More complex than structured programming
Slight performance overhead
Requires careful design

Real-life analogy
=================
Think of OOP like:
A car system

Class = Car blueprint
Objects = Toyota, BMW, Tesla
Properties = color, speed
Behaviors = drive, brake

Structured vs OOP (quick comparison)
====================================
Feature	                Structured	                      OOP
=======                 ==========                        ====
Focus	                Functions	                      Objects
Approach	            Top-down	                      Bottom-up
Data security	        Low	                              High
Reusability	            Limited	                          High


1.3 Characteristics of object oriented languages
================================================
Object-oriented languages like C++, Java, Python are designed around objects and classes. 
Their main characteristics are based on how they model real-world systems.

1. Class and Object
===================
Class → Blueprint or template
Object → Instance of a class

- Everything in OOP is built using objects.

2. Encapsulation (Data Hiding)
==============================
Wrapping data and methods together into a single unit (class)
Restricts direct access to data using access modifiers (private)

- Improves security and control over data

3. Abstraction
===============
Hiding implementation details and showing only essential features
Focuses on what to do, not how it is done

- Example: You use a phone without knowing internal circuits

4. Inheritance
==============
One class acquires properties and behaviors of another class
Promotes code reusability

class Animal {
public:
    void eat() {
        cout << "Eating";
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal {
};

5. Polymorphism
===============
“One name, many forms”
Same function behaves differently in different contexts

Types:
Compile-time (function overloading)
Run-time (function overriding)

6. Dynamic Binding
==================
Method call is resolved at runtime (not compile time)
Used in runtime polymorphism

- Achieved using virtual functions in C++

7. Message Passing
==================
Objects communicate by sending messages (calling methods)
obj.show();

8. Modularity
=============
Program is divided into independent modules (classes)
Easier to maintain and debug

Summary Table
=============
Characteristic	            Meaning
==============              =======
Class & Object	            Building blocks
Encapsulation	              Data protection
Abstraction	                Hide complexity
Inheritance	                Code reuse
Polymorphism	              Multiple forms
Dynamic Binding	            Runtime method decision
Message Passing	            Object communication
Modularity	                Divided structure

Simple idea
===========
OOP languages model software like a real-world system made of interacting objects.


